# PART 40 — STRICT DEVELOPMENT RULES

These instructions are mandatory.

### Rule 1 — Understand Before Building

Inspect the current project files and runtime environment before modifying anything.

If the project is empty, create the required structure.

If the project already exists, preserve working functionality and inspect the architecture first.

### Rule 2 — No Destructive Changes

Do not overwrite working modules without understanding their dependencies.

Do not delete useful files without justification.

Do not reset real database data.

Do not expose secrets.

### Rule 3 — No Fake Functionality

Never claim a feature works unless it has been implemented and tested.

A button must perform a real action.

A form must save valid data.

A table must read actual stored data.

A workflow must execute its defined steps.

A report must derive from real records.

### Rule 4 — No Unnecessary Duplication

Do not create duplicate components, models, tables or business logic.

Reuse shared utilities and design components.

### Rule 5 — Type Safety

Use TypeScript strict mode.

Avoid unnecessary use of `any`.

Define shared types and API contracts.

### Rule 6 — Backend Validation

Every sensitive business rule must be enforced on the server.

Never rely on frontend validation alone.

### Rule 7 — Database Integrity

Use migrations.

Use foreign keys.

Use transactions.

Use constraints.

Use proper indexing.

### Rule 8 — Permission Enforcement

Permissions must be checked on backend endpoints and database access paths.

Hiding a button is not sufficient authorization.

### Rule 9 — Quality Before Quantity

Build a smaller number of fully functional modules before creating dozens of incomplete pages.

### Rule 10 — Maintain Context

Keep the approved architecture and decisions available throughout the project.

Do not change the color palette, business model, data ownership or naming conventions randomly between tasks.

### Rule 11 — Honest Reporting

At the end of each implementation phase, report:

- What was implemented.
- What is functional.
- What was tested.
- What remains incomplete.
- Which integrations are actually connected.
- Which settings require manual configuration.
- Any technical limitations.
- Known risks.

### Rule 12 — Do Not Stop at Static UI

After implementing a screen, connect it to its real backend service and database.

If the backend is not yet available, clearly mark the screen as incomplete, not production-ready.

